C/ Self-CANCEL (bare `CANCEL;` inside the task's own body) -- the key
C/ distinction from TERMINATE (USA003087 Sec. 23.6): CANCEL is *graceful*.
C/ The current cycle runs to completion (so the WRITE after CANCEL DOES
C/ execute); only the next re-arm of this cyclic (REPEAT EVERY) task is
C/ suppressed.  Expected: BEFORE / AFTER CANCEL / DONE -- printed once, not
C/ repeating.  (A TERMINATE in the same spot would abort the cycle and the
C/ "AFTER CANCEL" line would never print.)
 TSELFCAN: PROGRAM;
    A: TASK;
       WRITE(6) 'IN A';
       CANCEL;
       WRITE(6) 'AFTER CANCEL';
    CLOSE A;
    WRITE(6) 'BEFORE';
    SCHEDULE A PRIORITY(80), REPEAT EVERY 1.0;
    WAIT 3.0;
    WRITE(6) 'DONE';
 CLOSE TSELFCAN;
